      "text": "To determine the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats at a round table with nine people, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of ways to arrange the nine people around a round table.**\n   The number of ways to arrange \\( n \\) people in a circle is \\((n-1)!\\). For 9 people, this is:\n   \\[\n   (9-1)! = 8! = 40320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Treat the four math majors as a single \"block\" or \"super person.\"**\n   This means we now have 6 \"people\" to arrange around the table (the block of 4 math majors, plus the 3 physics majors and the 2 biology majors).\n   The number of ways to arrange 6 people in a circle is:\n   \\[\n   (6-1)! = 5! = 120\n   \\]\n\n3. **Arrange the 4 math majors within their block.**\n   The 4 math majors can be arranged among themselves in:\n   \\[\n   4! = 24\n   \\]\n   ways.\n\n4. **Calculate the total number of favorable arrangements.**\n   The total number of favorable arrangements is the product of the number of ways to arrange the 6 \"people\" in a circle and the number of ways to arrange the 4 math majors within their block:\n   \\[\n   5! \\times 4! = 120 \\times 24 = 2880\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the probability.**\n   The probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is the number of favorable arrangements divided by the total number of arrangements:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\n\\]",
